<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermann Hesse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermann <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> was a German-born Swiss novelist and poet, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calw">Calw</a> in Württemberg. His novels, including Siddhartha (1922), Steppenwolf (1927) and The Glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">Bead</a> Game (1943), explore spiritual search and individual identity. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and opposed German militarism, received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1946, and found a vast new readership in the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse.jpg" alt="Hermann Hesse" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Hesse_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hermann Karl Hesse was born on 2 July 1877 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calw">Calw</a>, in Württemberg. His grandparents served at a Basel Mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and his mother, Marie Gundert, was born at a mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>. The family lived in Basel from 1881 for six years before returning to Calw. In 1891 Hesse entered the Evangelical Theological Seminary at Maulbronn Abbey, where a serious personal crisis began. In March 1892 he fled the seminary and was found in a field a day later; after an attempt at suicide in May he spent time in institutions, and in 1893 he passed the examination that ended his schooling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 17 October 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> began work in a bookshop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, and after twelve-hour days he read theology, Goethe, Lessing and Schiller, and from 1895 Nietzsche. In 1904 he married Maria Bernoulli and settled in Gaienhofen on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, where he wrote Beneath the Wheel, published in 1906.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> broke out in 1914 he registered as a volunteer and was assigned to the care of prisoners of war. On 3 November he published an essay, &quot;O <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a>, Not These Tones&quot;, urging intellectuals not to fall for nationalistic hatred. The German press attacked him, though Theodor Heuss and Romain Rolland supported him. By 1919 his marriage had fallen apart, and he resettled alone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>, moving to Montagnola on 11 May. Siddhartha appeared in 1922. He married Ruth Wenger in 1924, divorced in 1927 and married Ninon in 1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>'s best-known novels are Demian, Steppenwolf, Siddhartha, Narcissus and Goldmund and The Glass Bead Game, each exploring an individual's search for authenticity, self-knowledge and spirituality. His interest in Eastern religious and philosophical traditions, and in Jungian analysis, shaped his work. He wrote that for different people there are different ways to God. He was widely read in the German-speaking world in his lifetime and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1946. His works were little read in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> when he died in 1962, but in the mid-1960s they became bestsellers there in paperback, a revival credited to their association with themes of the counterculture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hermann Hesse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Swabian school of poets, including Ludwig Uhland, Justinus Kerner and Eduard Mörike, gave the kingdom a literary reputation, and the novelist <b>Hermann Hesse</b> was born at Calw in 1877.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The book had a notable impact on the 1960s counterculture and on figures such as John Cage, Jorge Luis Borges and <b>Hermann Hesse</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In his short story &quot;Chagrin d'amour&quot; (1908), <b>Hermann Hesse</b> invented a medieval origin for it, giving it to an obscure troubadour who sings it to a queen he loves without hope.</blockquote><a href="/songs/plaisir-damour">Plaisir d'amour</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calw">Calw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Montagnola, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.71,8.74&amp;year=1932" aria-label="Calw on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4752 462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hermann Hesse"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5202 732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5202 732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.71,8.74&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
